<title>BELGIUM: Spain's Rojo paints rosy economic picture.</title>
<headline>Spain's Rojo paints rosy economic picture.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-07-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Spain's central bank governor Luis Angel Rojo, painting a rosy picture of the country's economy, said on Wednesday its budgetary policy was sustainable and the public deficit was set to fall sharply as a percentage of gross domestic product (GDP).</p>
<p>&quot;Before the year 2000, we will have a public deficit of 1.5 or 1.6 of GDP,&quot; he told a European Parliament committee.  </p>
<p>He also said that Spain's inflation rate was set to stay stable at about two percent this year and would remain that way in 1998.</p>
<p>&quot;We don't think it will go over two percent next year,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>On overall growth, Rojo said April's increase in GDP had been at an annual rate of about 3.0 percent but that he did not see or want the economy to be too robust.</p>
<p>&quot;I don't think we can expect very rapid growth. We don't want any extreme growth in the coming months.&quot;</p>
</text>
